4. FORKS AND CONTRIBUTIONS

   Because the Repository is public on GitHub, GitHub's Terms of Service give
   every user the technical ability to view and fork it. That platform-level
   ability does not, by itself, grant any right to use, keep or publish the
   copy so created. Accordingly, for as long as the Repository is public:

   (a) a fork is permitted only as the technical means of preparing a
       contribution to be submitted back to the Repository as a pull request;

   (b) such a fork must not be modified beyond what the intended contribution
       requires, must not be advertised, distributed or presented as an
       independent project, and should be deleted once the pull request has
       been merged or closed;

   (c) any other use of a fork — including simply keeping it publicly
       available as a standalone copy of the Software — constitutes a
       distribution and is prohibited by Section 3.

   By submitting a contribution to the Repository, You grant the Owner a
   perpetual, worldwide, irrevocable, royalty-free and sublicensable license
   to use, modify, publish and distribute that contribution as part of the
